WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.370
I am still in the Arctic, so today’s guest
video comes from Chase at ScienceC,

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:07.649
who sent in the best pitch out of all the
150 that I got.

00:00:07.649 --> 00:00:09.590
And it started, “hi Tom,

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:12.710
I’ve got two of my sister’s teeth dissolving
in soda”.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:14.280
I have a confession to make.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.680
I love Mountain Dew.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.289
I think my weather balloon video alone probably
took a few 12-packs.

00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:25.159
Mountain Dew isn’t exactly known to be the
healthiest beverage out there.

00:00:25.159 --> 00:00:30.099
So today, I’m going to do a little experiment
to see just how bad Mountain Dew is for your

00:00:30.099 --> 00:00:31.099
teeth.

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:33.109
So right here, I have two of my sister’s
molars.

00:00:33.109 --> 00:00:36.190
I’m going to be soaking them in both a bottle
of Mountain Dew,

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.980
and a bottle of Coca-Cola for the next three
weeks.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.899
So let’s see what happens!

00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:46.699
These teeth have been soaking in the soda
for two weeks and five days.

00:00:46.699 --> 00:00:48.500
Before we take them out and weigh them,

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.510
let’s get a little background on this soda.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:55.039
So the Mountain Dew right here has a pH of
around 3.1

00:00:55.039 --> 00:00:59.190
whereas the Coca-Cola has a pH of 2.5

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:02.980
making it six times more acidic than the Mountain
Dew.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:06.860
So let’s take these teeth out, and see how
much their mass has changed.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:11.060
I’m going to pour the Mountain Dew out here,
and get the tooth out…

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:15.030
I got the tooth out, and as you can see, it
looks a lot more yellower,

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.840
especially at the base of it, than before.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.640
Whoa, whoa, whoa...

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.920
This tooth does not look very good at all.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.590
It is all black around the crown,

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:27.409
and it just looks disgusting and nasty.

00:01:27.409 --> 00:01:33.370
So I’m going to clean it up with a little
bit of water.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:36.799
So now that I’ve gotten these teeth dried
off, if you look really closely,

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:39.829
you can actually see that a lot of the enamel
that was there

00:01:39.829 --> 00:01:43.409
— the white stuff — is gone where it previously
was before.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:45.910
And on the Coca-Cola tooth, you can’t really
see a whole lot,

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:46.910
because it’s so stained.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:49.909
Now the teeth are sufficiently dried off,
let’s weigh them.

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:53.110
So the Coca-Cola tooth weighs .80 g,

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:58.079
meaning that it’s .06 g lighter than it
was before.

00:01:58.079 --> 00:02:05.490
Now, if you look at the Mountain Dew tooth,
we can see that it is .81 g,

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:12.220
meaning that it is 13 hundredths of a gram
lighter than it was before.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:16.260
Now if we adjust for the initial mass of the
teeth,

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:21.030
we can see that the Mountain Dew tooth lost
14% of its mass,

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:25.030
while the Coca-Cola tooth only lost 7% of
its mass.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.590
Half of what the Mountain Dew lost.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:30.620
Since Coca-Cola is six times more acidic than
Mountain Dew,

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:35.040
you would expect it to have a higher decay
rate than Mountain Dew.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.300
This is not the case for two factors.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:42.010
And that first factor is titratable acidity,
or TA.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.880
So pH is a measure of the level of acidity.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.489
That means that Coca-Cola is more acidic than
Mountain Dew.

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:54.940
However, Mountain Dew has a higher titratable
acidity than Coca-Cola.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:58.099
Meaning that it has more acid than Coca-Cola.

00:02:58.099 --> 00:03:03.099
This means that it takes longer for the saliva
in your mouth to dilute that acid

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:05.160
than it would for most cola drinks.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.480
In addition to this,

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.409
it’s also the type of acid that’s added
that makes the difference.

00:03:10.409 --> 00:03:13.220
So Mountain Dew, just like most non-cola beverages,

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.620
has citric acid added into it.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:18.500
And citric acid is an organic acid,

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.340
meaning that it will be able to break down
the calcium in your teeth

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:27.049
faster than inorganic acids like the phosphoric
acid found in most cola beverages.

00:03:27.049 --> 00:03:31.770
This ultimately means that Mountain Dew is
worse for your teeth than Coca-Cola,

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.220
even though Coca-Cola is way more acidic

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:37.319
and that’s something that you might not
have known.

00:03:37.319 --> 00:03:38.319
Thanks, Tom.

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:39.319
Thanks, Chase!

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:41.040
Thought I’d left that catchphrase behind.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.140
Go subscribe to his channel, he’s got some
wonderful videos coming up,

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:45.790
links on screen or in the description now.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:46.790
Let’s face it,

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:49.800
he’s going to have my job in 5 or 10 years’
time, you’re getting in early.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:53.909
Next time, we have, well, a very familiar
part of China.

